course: everyday
lesson: 318
title: Drinking water, cuts, bruises, head bumps, burns, growing and needing glasses
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Your body needs water to work well. When you are thirsty, water is the best drink, and you need more on hot days and when you run about.
explain: Small injuries have simple first aid. A cut is washed, pressed and covered; a bruise gets a wrapped cold pack; a burn is cooled under cool running water for 20 minutes.
explain: Some injuries look small but need an adult. After a hard knock on the head, tell an adult even if you feel fine, because a head injury needs watching for a while.
explain: Bodies grow at their own pace, and eyes are different too. If you cannot read the board at school, tell a teacher and a parent: an eye test may show you need glasses.
fact: When you are thirsty, drink water; children need about six to eight cups a day, and more when it is hot or they are active.
fact: Your body needs water to work well.
fact: For a small cut or scraped knee, wash it with clean water, press with a clean cloth to stop the bleeding, then cover it with a plaster or bandage.
fact: Cleaning a cut stops infection, and covering it keeps dirt out.
fact: For a bruise, put a cold pack wrapped in a cloth on it for a few minutes, because cold reduces swelling and pain.
fact: Never put ice straight on the skin; wrap it in a cloth first.
fact: After hitting your head hard, tell an adult even if you feel fine, because a head injury needs watching.
fact: If you touch something hot, cool the burn under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult.
fact: Cooling a burn stops it going deeper into the skin; ice, butter and creams do not help.
fact: Everyone grows at their own pace, and growth spurts come at different ages.
fact: If you cannot see the board at school, tell a teacher and a parent, because you may need an eye test.
exercise: You come in from football on a hot day and feel very thirsty. What is the best drink? | Water | A fizzy drink | A milkshake | A sugary energy drink
exercise: About how much water does a child need each day? | About six to eight cups, more when hot or active | About one cup a day is plenty | Only what is in your food | Twenty cups or more, because the more you drink the better you grow
exercise: Why does your body need water? | Water helps every part of your body work well | Water only keeps your skin clean and stops it drying out | Water is only needed when you are ill | Water makes muscles grow bigger
exercise: A girl in Sydney scrapes her knee on the footpath. What should she do first? | Wash it with clean water | Cover it at once, dirt and all | Rub some sand off and leave it | Put butter on it
exercise: After washing a small cut, it is still bleeding. What should you do? | Press a clean cloth on it, then cover it with a plaster | Leave it open so the air can dry it out and form a scab quickly | Wash it again and again for an hour | Put a hot cloth on it
exercise: Why should a cut be washed and covered? | Cleaning stops infection and covering keeps dirt out | So it does not itch at night | So nobody can see it | Plasters make the cut close up at once, so it heals overnight
exercise: A boy bumps his leg on the table and a bruise starts to come up. What helps? | A cold pack wrapped in a cloth for a few minutes | A hot water bottle pressed straight on the skin until it warms up | Rubbing it hard for ten minutes | Ice pressed on bare skin for an hour
exercise: Why does a cold pack help a bruise? | Cold reduces swelling and pain | Cold kills the germs inside it | Cold changes its colour back at once | It only helps if the bruise is old
exercise: A girl falls off the climbing frame and hits her head hard, but says she feels fine. What should she do? | Tell an adult anyway | Keep playing, she feels fine | Go home alone and lie down | Wait to see if it hurts tomorrow
exercise: Why should you tell an adult about a hard knock on the head, even if you feel fine? | A head injury needs watching, as problems can show later | Head bumps always need stitches, even when there is no blood at all | Adults like to know every small thing | So the playground can be closed
exercise: A boy touches a hot iron and burns his finger. What should he do? | Cool it under cool running water for 20 minutes and tell an adult | Spread butter on it straight away to keep the air out of the burn | Hold ice on it for an hour | Wrap it tightly and say nothing
exercise: Why should a burn be cooled under running water? | Cooling stops the burn going deeper | Water washes the burn away | It is only to stop it hurting for a second | Wet skin heals into a scar faster
exercise: A girl worries because she is much shorter than her friends. What is TRUE? | Everyone grows at their own pace | Short children never grow taller | She must be ill | Eating more will make her grow at once
exercise: Why can two friends of the same age be very different heights? | Growth spurts come at different ages | One of them must sleep less | Height is set by how much you exercise | They drink different amounts of milk
exercise: A boy cannot read the words on the board, even from his seat near the front. What should he do? | Tell his teacher and a parent | Copy from his neighbour every day | Squint and say nothing | Sit at the back so nobody notices
exercise: What might happen if he tells them? | He may get an eye test and perhaps glasses | He will be moved to a lower class | Nothing can be done about eyes | He will be told to try harder
test: On a hot camping trip in Texas, which plan keeps a child's body working well? | Drinking water often, more than on a cool day | Drinking only at mealtimes | Drinking less to avoid stopping for toilets | Drinking fizzy drinks instead of water
test: A boy grazes his elbow on gravel and puts a plaster straight over the dirt. What did he forget? | To wash it with clean water first | To put butter on it | To make it bleed more | To keep it in the sun
test: Your friend says to put ice cubes straight on a bruise for an hour. What is better? | A cold pack wrapped in a cloth for a few minutes | Ice cubes on bare skin for even longer, so the bruise freezes | A hot towel to warm the bruise | Rubbing the bruise very hard
test: In the playground two children bump heads hard. One cries, the other says, «I'm fine.» Which is TRUE? | Both should tell an adult, because head injuries need watching | Only the one who is crying needs help, because the other would feel it | Neither needs help if there is no blood | The one who is fine can keep playing alone
test: A girl in New York splashes hot water from the kettle on her hand. Which first aid is right? | Cool running water for 20 minutes, and tell an adult | Rub butter into the burn so the skin stays soft | Press an ice pack on it hard | Cover it with toothpaste
test: Which is TRUE about a burn? | Cooling it under water stops it going deeper | A burn only hurts, it never gets worse | Creams are better than water at first | Blowing on it is enough
test: Last year Sam was the shortest in class; this year he grew 8 centimetres. What does this show? | People have growth spurts at different ages | Sam must have been very ill last year and is now catching up | Height can never change quickly | He must have stretched every night
test: A girl gets headaches because she screws up her eyes to see the board. What is the best next step? | Tell a teacher and parent so she can get an eye test | Move further from the board | Take a headache pill every morning before school and carry on | Close one eye when she reads
test: Which is the right first aid for a small cut? | Wash, press with a clean cloth, then cover | Cover, then wash later at home | Leave it open and keep playing | Put a hot cloth on it to stop the bleeding
explain: A girl in Colorado's scraped knee was not cleaned after a fall; a few days later it was red, hot and swollen, and she needed antibiotics.
explain: A boy in Australia did not drink anything on a long, hot hike; he got a headache, felt sick and nearly fainted before his mum gave him water in the shade.
fact: A dirty cut can get infected, turning red, hot and swollen.
fact: In hot weather the body loses lots of water as sweat, and not drinking can make you very ill.
test: scraped my knee, its dirty but whatever | Wash it with clean water and cover it | Leave it, dirt protects it | Put mud on it | Rub it with a leaf
test: knee cut is red n hot n puffy 3 days later | Tell an adult; it may be infected | It's normal, ignore it | Squeeze it hard | Pick off the scab
test: put ice straight on my bruise? | Wrap it in a cloth first | Yes, straight on | Yes, for an hour | Use hot water instead
test: did u hear a kid didn't drink anything at the footy carnival in the heat and "collapsed and had to go on a drip". lesson? | Drink water, more when it's hot or you're active | Only drink when you finish playing, so you don't get a stitch | Water slows you down in sport | Fizzy drinks work better than water
test: my nan says putting ice straight on a bruise "freezes the skin off". what's the true bit | Never put ice straight on skin; wrap it in a cloth | Ice should always go straight on | Bruises need heat, not cold, so put a hot towel on | Bruises need nothing at all
test: kids say the girl with glasses "is half blind, four eyes". pass it on? | No, needing glasses is normal and teasing is unkind | Yes, it's just a joke | Yes, and hide her glasses | Yes, it's true
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the correct treatment of a small cut? | Washing with clean water, pressing with a clean cloth, then covering | Leaving it uncovered, so that it may dry in the open air and form a scab | Covering it without washing | Applying butter to seal it
exam: Identify the principal sign that a cut has become infected. | It turns red, hot and swollen | It forms a dry scab | It stops bleeding | It becomes slightly itchy while healing
exam: It is given that a burn has been sustained from a hot object. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | It shall be cooled under cool running water for twenty minutes | Butter shall be applied at once, so as to seal the skin from the air | Ice shall be pressed directly upon it | A thick cream shall be spread over it
exam: State the approximate daily quantity of water required by children. | About six to eight cups, more when hot or active | About one cup in total | About twenty cups | None, if food is eaten
